<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles II, Elector Palatine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a>–<a href="/years/1685">1685</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> from 1680 to 1685, the last of the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> line. On his childless death the electorate passed to the Catholic Philip William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> claimed part of the inheritance for Charles's sister Elizabeth Charlotte, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. The dispute was one of the causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, in which French troops devastated the Palatinate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-elector-palatine.jpg" alt="Charles II, Elector Palatine" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem Wissing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_II,_Elector_Palatine_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
